<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Crown Colony of Sarawak</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – 1963 CE · capital Kuching</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British colony in northern Borneo formed when the last White Rajah ceded Sarawak, despite strong local protest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-british-borneo">Japanese occupation of British Borneo</a>, Rajah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vyner-brooke">Charles Vyner Brooke</a> ceded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/raj-of-sarawak">Raj of Sarawak</a> to the British crown on 1 July 1946, arguing that he could not fund reconstruction. The cession was narrowly approved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-negri">Council Negri</a> but opposed by many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> and members of the Brooke family.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was run by a governor with an appointed and later partly elected council. The anti-cession movement culminated in the assassination of Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duncan-stewart-colonial-administrator">Duncan Stewart</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibu">Sibu</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosli-dhobi">Rosli Dhobi</a> in December 1949. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> invested in schools, health and roads, and oil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> exports grew. Communist influence spread among Chinese youth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cobbold-commission">Cobbold Commission</a> found majority support, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/raj-of-sarawak">Sarawak</a> joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a> on 16 September 1963 with safeguards on immigration, language and religion. The Indonesian Confrontation followed, and Sarawak remains a state of Malaysia.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/crown-colony-of-sarawak.jpg" alt="Crown Colony of Sarawak" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Cypp0847 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sarawak_(1947%E2%80%931963).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – 1963 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuching</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duncan Stewart, Anthony Abell</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Sunni Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, Iban, English, Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Sarawak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1954&amp;focus=crown-colony-of-sarawak" aria-label="Crown Colony of Sarawak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7745 2119 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Crown Colony of Sarawak lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8065 2375L8065 2375M8065 2375L4997 651M8065 2375L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8065 2375h0M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8065 2375h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8065 2375h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kuching. <a href="/atlas?year=1954&amp;focus=crown-colony-of-sarawak">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
